The developer has finished a round of work on branch '{{ .branch }}' in response to PR feedback.

Brief given to the developer:
{{ .developer_feedback }}

Inspect the latest commits and diffs on the branch. For each requirement in the brief verify:
- Was the change implemented?
- Is the implementation correct and complete?
- Does it actually address the underlying PR comment?

If every requirement is fully satisfied:
  Call submit_outcome with outcome "approved".
  Summarise what was done in the reason field. Do NOT include any approval, denial, or sign-off language — only a concise summary of the actual code changes and any relevant technical notes (e.g., additional fixes or decisions taken outside the workstream scope). The summary becomes the branch's validated requirement set for the push step.

## Post-strip supersedence (KB-151)

When the developer's work this round removed commits (a strip or reset a
reviewer ruling required), the ruling supersedes any earlier brief item that
required carrying those commits. Validating a post-strip state:
- The absence of a stripped commit is NOT a missing requirement. Verify the
  strip itself instead (the commit is gone from the branch, no residue).
- Do NOT route needs_work for a stripped commit's absence and do not
  re-require it.
- The coordinator records the stripped requirement (SHA + subject) for a
  separate card; the requirement is tracked there, not on this branch.

If any requirements are missing or only partially addressed:
  Call submit_outcome with outcome "needs_work".
  In the reason field, write a precise brief covering only what is still missing — the developer will receive this directly. Again, no approval or denial language; only the remaining changes needed.

## Convergence (KB-265)
When the reviewer previously approved this state and no new commits landed that change anything relevant to the brief, do NOT route needs_work for process reasons: a validator that demands fresh commits on a finished, reviewer-approved, CI-green diff creates an unterminable loop. If the state satisfies the brief, approve; needs_work is for ACTUAL outstanding requirements. The round budget and the branch manager's final say sit downstream of this verdict.
